+# Auditor Report — Post-Rename Hybrid Tool Registry (Live Audit)
+
+**Cycle**: 2026-10-29 22:45+08, Job 477792, role: auditor
+**Finding issue**: #2640 (open — fix is controller-side)
+**Baseline context**: #2488 reported the naming divergence (landed
+report-only); the rename deployed same day as the "#2454 r3" deploy, per
+in-repo comment annotations. This audit answers the question the fix left
+open: what does the post-rename surface look like to an external caller?
+
+## Method
+
+One `tools/list` read (151 tools), ten single-shot name probes across both
+naming schemes, one `bridge.work_order` read. Every probe was read-only or
+deliberately invalid-argument; zero state was mutated. The breakage evidence
+is not synthetic: this same agent runtime lived through the rename today,
+calling `github_create_issue` successfully at 11:48+08 (issue #2431) and
+hitting `-32602 Tool not found` on the identical name at 22:52+08, with the
+dotted successor used instead. `bridge_work_queue` shows the same arc (alive
+08:45, dead 22:50), while `bridge_submit_patch` (underscore) was still alive
+at 23:05+08 — this cycle's own registration PR (#2635) went through it.
+
+## A-1 — the registry is a hybrid, not an SSOT
+
+The published tool surface now carries both naming schemes simultaneously.
+Dotted canonicals exist for the whole github family and the queue/order
+reads (`github.create_issue`, `bridge.work_queue`, `bridge.work_order`, …).
+Residual underscore registrations exist for the submission and feedback
+family (`bridge_submit`, `bridge_submit_patch`, `bridge_read_feedback`,
+`bridge_work`, `bridge_work_renew`, `bridge_work_release`, `work_claim`).
+The two schemes are not aliases of each other: probing
+`bridge_work_queue` returns `-32602 Tool not found` while
+`bridge.work_queue` answers on the same session — separate registrations,
+no cross-resolution. The error classes themselves encode the split: dead
+names answer "Tool not found", live-but-misused names answer "Input
+validation error: Invalid arguments" (observed on
+`bridge_read_feedback`/`bridge_work_renew`/`bridge_work_release` with empty
+arguments). A caller discovering names through `tools/list` therefore sees
+two plausible spellings for the same operation with only one live, and
+nothing in the surface distinguishes canonical from vestigial.
+
+## A-2 — the breakage class is real, same-day, and silent
+
+The rename shipped with no deprecation window and no redirect. A caller that
+cached a morning `tools/list` (or hardcoded the morning's working name)
+fails mid-cycle with a bare `-32602` that does not mention the replacement.
+Repo-side callers are safe — this audit verified that all five in-repo
+references to the old names are comment annotations documenting the rename
+(`continuous_agent_loop.py`, `bridge_landing_watchdog.py`,
+`radar_index_freshness_guard.py`, `tool_consolidation_reference.py`,
+`test_dispatch_honesty_floor.py`), with zero call sites — but the bridge
+exists precisely for external agent runtimes, and those are the callers that
+break. The symptom is also misleading: "Tool not found" reads as a typo or
+an outage, not as a rename, which wastes a debugging cycle per affected
+caller.
+
+## A-3 — the #2488 four are still dead, now without an owner
+
+The four tools #2488 named dead (`bridge.read_feedback`,
+`bridge.work.renew`, `bridge.work.release`, `work.claim` — dotted, as the
+guide named them) all still return `-32602 Tool not found` after the rename
+deploy. The fix chose the SSOT path and renamed the live plane; the dead
+names were left as bare removals. They are the standing monument of A-2:
+every future rename will mint more of them unless removals become
+self-describing.
+
+## Recommendation — tombstones, not aliases
+
+Do not resurrect aliases (that recreates the divergence #2488 documented).
+Register removed names as permanent tombstones whose only behavior is a
+structured response — `{"error": "tool_moved", "use": "<canonical name>"}` —
+or, minimally, enrich the `-32602` path with a nearest-registry-match
+suggestion. Tombstones are not callable in any useful sense, so they add no
+surface ambiguity; they convert silent breakage into a one-hop redirect, and
+they retroactively heal the four #2488 names plus every future rename. Pair
+this with a registry-version field in the `tools/list` result metadata so
+callers can detect "the surface changed since my last discovery" in one
+comparison. Both changes are controller-side (R49 lane); no repo PR can
+reach them.
+
+## Dedup and disclosure
+
+#2488 is the pre-fix divergence finding (closed by the rename deploy); R67
+and its fix #2492 cover the default-repo caller migration — a different
+plane; this lane is the post-rename residual: the hybrid surface, the
+witnessed breakage class, and the tombstone mechanism. No prior lane covers
+any of the three. Total live calls this cycle: twelve, all read-only or
+intentionally invalid; no writes, no credentials, no load.
